# TryHackMe — Extract Room Writeup

**Target:** `10.48.150.125`
**Application:** TryBookMe (Online Library)
**Vulnerabilities chained:** SSRF via `preview.php` → internal port scan → Gopher-tunneled SSRF → CVE-2025-29927 (Next.js middleware auth bypass) → PHP session cookie (2FA flag) tampering

---

## 1. Reconnaissance

### Nmap

```
nmap -sC -sV -v -p- 10.48.150.125
```

Open ports: `22/tcp` (SSH), `80/tcp` (HTTP). Nothing unusual at the network layer — the interesting surface is entirely in the web app.

### Source review

The app (`TryBookMe - Online Library`) is a static Bootstrap page listing PDFs. Each "Preview" link calls:

```js
function openPdf(url) {
  const iframe = document.getElementById('pdfFrame');
  iframe.src = 'preview.php?url=' + encodeURIComponent(url);
  iframe.style.display = 'block';
}
```

`preview.php?url=` takes a fully attacker-controlled URL and fetches it server-side to render in an iframe. That is the entire attack surface of this box — everything below is built on this one parameter.

### Directory enumeration

```
ffuf -u 'http://10.48.150.125/FUZZ' \
  -w /usr/share/seclists/Discovery/Web-Content/DirBuster-2007_directory-list-2.3-small.txt \
  -mc all -t 100 -ic -fc 404 -e .php
```

| Path | Status |
|---|---|
| `/` (index.php) | 200 |
| `/preview.php` | 200 (19B empty response without a `url` param) |
| `/management/` | 301 |
| `/pdf/` | 301 |
| `/javascript/` | 301 |

`dirsearch` confirmed the same three directories (`/pdf`, `/management`, `/javascript`). `/management/` is the one worth coming back to later — it's what Flag 2 lives behind.

